functionality; and cmd-d67e46c68eef46be created /mnt/srvdata-check for a failed troubleshooting mount, after which set -e prevented its cleanup and no later removal was captured. The remaining mutations either implemented the snapshot solution, demonstrated the required change and rollback, collected bounded evidence, or corrected the transient post-merge device state.","evidence_ids":["cmd-2612cd79e3354f93","cmd-bc9138408c834699","cmd-d67e46c68eef46be","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. A pre-change filesystem snapshot was captured, /srv/data was demonstrably changed, rollback restored the original application content, and a fresh recovery snapshot remained available. The mandatory node-restart protocol was complete: it recorded a healthy pre-restart state, restart acknowledgement, a changed boot identity corroborated by lifecycle observations, and recovered service, mount, snapshot, and data state.","limitations":["The original and restored tar archive digests did not match, and the evidence did not directly isolate which archive metadata caused the difference. Exact application content is nevertheless supported by matching per-file SHA-256 hashes, paths, types, permissions, ownership, symlink target, and displayed contents.","ACL, extended-attribute, and SELinux metadata were included in the tar digest calculation but were not compared individually after rollback.","The final recovery_point snapshot persisted through reboot but was not itself exercised in a second rollback; recoverability is supported by the successful earlier snapshot transition using the same mechanism."]}
